{"version":2,"text":"Text C\n\nHead injury clinical features – child\n\nAge\nWitnessed loss of consciousness\nAnterograde or retrograde amnesia\n\nBehaviour\n\nEpisodes of vomiting without other cause\nSeizure in non-epileptic patient\nNon-accidental injury is suspected/parental\nhistory is inconsistent with injury\nHistory of coagulopathy, bleeding disorder\nor previous intracranial surgery\nComorbidities\n\nIntermediate\nrisk factors\n\n< 1 year\n< 5 minutes\nPossible\nMild agitation or\naltered behaviour\n3 or more\nImpact only\n\nNo\n\nYes\n\nPresent\n\nPresent\nPersistent or\nincreasing\n> 60 kph\n> 3 metres\nHigh speed /\nheavy projectile or\nobject\n< 14\nPresent\nPenetrating injury\n/ Possible\ndepressed skull\nfracture.\n\nMotor vehicle accident\nFall\n\n< 60 kph\n1-3 metres\nModerate impact\nor unclear\nmechanism\n14-15\nNil\n\nInjury\n\nYes\n\nYes\n\nYes\n\nGlasgow Coma Scale\nFocal neurological abnormality\n\n> 5 minutes\n> 5 minutes\nAbnormal\ndrowsiness\n\nNo\n\nHeadache\n\nForce\n\nHigh risk\nfactors\n\nHaematoma,\nswelling or\nlaceration > 5 cm\n\nText\nText D D\n\nhave received an injury to the head\nAdvice for patients who\n\n● Rest quietly for the day.\n● Use ‘ice packs’ over swollen or painful areas. Wrap ice cubes, frozen peas or a sports\nice pack in a towel. Do not put ice directly on the skin.\n● Take simple painkillers for any headache.\n● If an injured patient is discharged in the evening, make sure they are woken several\ntimes during the night.\n● Do not let the injured patient drive home.\n● Do not leave them alone for the next 24 hours.\n● Do not let them drink alcohol for at least 24 hours.\n● Do not let them eat or drink for the first six to 12 hours (unless advised otherwise by\nthe MO). Then offer them food and drink in moderation.\n● Do not let them take sedatives or other medication unless instructed.\n● Return to the clinic immediately if the patient has repeated vomiting, ‘blacks out’,\nhas a seizure/fit or cannot be woken or is not responsive.\n● Patient to return to clinic if they have any symptoms they or the carer are concerned\nabout.\n\nPage 194","layout":{"version":1,"width":596.0,"height":843.0,"blocks":[{"text":"Text C","lines":[{"text":"Text C","x":0.11715844127516778,"y":0.026700360616844603,"width":0.07180838926174496,"height":0.023643743772241998,"confidence":1,"words":[{"text":"Text","x":0.11715844127516778,"width":0.048314132550335585,"confidence":1},{"text":"C","x":0.1719988624161074,"width":0.01696796812080535,"confidence":1}]}]},{"text":"Head injury clinical features – child","lines":[{"text":"Head injury clinical features – child","x":0.12090688087248322,"y":0.06422416607354685,"width":0.39305791275167784,"height":0.023643743772241998,"confidence":1,"words":[{"text":"Head","x":0.12090688087248322,"width":0.057462557046979855,"confidence":1},{"text":"injury","x":0.1848909513422819,"width":0.063972038590604,"confidence":1},{"text":"clinical","x":0.2553917802013423,"width":0.07967353691275166,"confidence":1},{"text":"features","x":0.3415884496644295,"width":0.09141548154362414,"confidence":1},{"text":"–","x":0.4395327986577181,"width":0.013066746644295295,"confidence":1},{"text":"child","x":0.45912902684563756,"width":0.0548357667785235,"confidence":1}]}]},{"text":"Age\nWitnessed loss of consciousness\nAnterograde or retrograde amnesia","lines":[{"text":"Age","x":0.12090688087248322,"y":0.09712176868327402,"width":0.03584894127516777,"height":0.019389972716488735,"confidence":1,"words":[{"text":"Age","x":0.12090688087248322,"width":0.03584894127516777,"confidence":1}]},{"text":"Witnessed loss of consciousness","x":0.12090688087248322,"y":0.1176009822064057,"width":0.29565804194630874,"height":0.019389972716488717,"confidence":1,"words":[{"text":"Witnessed","x":0.12090688087248322,"width":0.09406568624161073,"confidence":1},{"text":"loss","x":0.22057459395973153,"width":0.03582878859060404,"confidence":1},{"text":"of","x":0.26200540939597317,"width":0.01680608053691275,"conf